; 1-Wire (DS18B20) master for the MVP chip (task B1-FW-onewire).
;
; The chip is the MASTER: the models in verif/vip/onewire.py are the slave
; (Ds18b20Target answers the reset with a presence pulse and drives read slots), so
; this program is the side that has to put a reset, time slots and a sample point on
; one open-drain line.
;
; Where the pin is
; ---------------
; One net, one pin, and it must be a uio pin: open drain is a direction, and only the
; uio pins have one (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Open drain`). The level register of that pin
; is never written, so it stays at its reset value 0 and the program only ever toggles
; the direction -- exactly what the RP2040 PIO does for an open-drain bus.
;
;   SET window, 1 pin, SET_BASE = DQ_PIN, SET_COUNT = 1
;       `set pindirs, 1` pulls the line low, `set pindirs, 0` releases it. This is the
;       only pin write in the program; there is no OUT window and no side-set, which
;       leaves the whole 5-bit delay field (0..31 SM cycles) for timing.
;   IN window, IN_BASE = DQ_PIN
;       `in pins, 1` samples the line, 25 SM cycles into every slot that has a short
;       low (a write-1 or a read slot). 1-Wire samples a read slot 15..60 us in
;       (DS18B20 "DS18B20 Samples 15 typ 30"), so the sample sits in the middle of
;       that window and inside the 16..66 us the slave model drives a read-0 over.
;   `wait` is not used: a PIO that cannot report anything to the host (there is no
;       IRQ in the MVP, docs/mvp-isa.md `## Differences from RP2040`) has nothing to do
;       with a level it can only see for itself, and every timing here is a delay.
;
; One loop for writing and for reading
; ------------------------------------
; A time slot is: pull the line low, release it, and (for a read) sample it 25 SM
; cycles in. The only difference between the three kinds of slot is how long the line
; stays low:
;
;   write 1   low 6 SM cycles (the DS18B20 tLOW1 window is 1..15 us)
;   write 0   low 65 SM cycles (tLOW0 is 60..120 us)
;   read      low 6 SM cycles, then the slave drives it or does not
;
; so the bit has to decide how long the low is, and the slot has to be 75 SM cycles
; long whichever bit it is. Both are one compare and two copies of the same pad:
;
;   out x, 1        the OSR bit lands in X and the OSR shifts right (OUT_SHIFTDIR=1),
;                   which is 1-Wire's own order: least significant bit first
;   jmp x-- w_zero  a taken JMP and a JMP that falls through both cost one SM cycle,
;                   so the `set pindirs, 1` that starts the low runs at the same offset
;                   of the slot in both branches. That is what keeps the period at 75
;                   SM cycles for a 0 and for a 1, which is what tSLOT measures.
;   w_one           release after 6 SM cycles and idle out the slot, sampling at 25
;   w_zero          hold the low for 65 SM cycles, then release (tREC)
;   jmp !osre w_slot   eight slots per TX word: PULL_THRESH is 8, `out x, 1` counts
;                   one bit per slot into osr_cnt, and `pull block` resets it to 0
;
; Read slots are write-1 slots whose bit happens to be 0 (a released line) and whose
; sample is kept: the host pushes a word of 0x00000000 per read byte, turns AUTOPUSH
; on and pushes ISR to the RX FIFO every eight samples, one byte per word. Nothing in
; IMEM has to know which of the two it is, which is what keeps the program inside 32
; words: a separate read loop would cost six more.
;
; Why this differs from an RP2040 one-wire program (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Open drain`,
; `## Differences from RP2040`):
;   * No `set gpio_inv, 1` (it does not exist and the assembler rejects the mnemonic):
;     the direction sense is already "1 pulls the line low", so nothing is inverted.
;   * No IRQ, so there is no `irq wait` to park in and no SMx_ADDR-based handshake
;     with an interrupt: the host sees the engine sitting on `pull block`, and that
;     instruction is the phase boundary the host synchronises to.
;   * 32 words of IMEM, so there is one bit loop instead of a write loop and a read
;     loop, and the two pads (reset, wait for the line) are a pair of `jmp x--` loops.
;
; The host protocol (fw/onewire/driver.py builds the words):
;   one 32-bit TX word per byte, bit k = the k-th slot of that byte, least significant
;   first, and 1 = pull the line low, because `out pindirs, 1` writes a direction. So
;   the host pushes the *inverted* byte (~b & 0xff): a 1-bit on the wire is a released
;   line, which is direction 0. A read byte is 0x00000000.
;   Y is unused. The engine blocks on `pull block` until the host pushes the next word,
;   which is how the host paces the engine and how it knows a byte boundary is safe.
; SHIFTCTRL: AUTOPULL off (the program pulls explicitly, and `pull block` is a no-op
; with autopull on and a full OSR), PULL_THRESH 8, OUT_SHIFTDIR 1, IN_SHIFTDIR 0 so a
; read byte lands in bits 7:0 in wire order, AUTOPUSH set by the host only for a read
; phase (see `read_phase` in the driver).

.program onewire
.wrap_target init
.wrap next_byte

.define PUBLIC DQ_PIN 0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The park. The line is released and stays that way, so the bus reads high and a host
; that has nothing to do leaves the engine here.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
init:
    set pindirs, 0            ; release: an open-drain line idles high
    jmp init

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A reset pulse, entered by forcing `jmp reset` (SMx_INSTR: SMx_ADDR is read-only and
; there is no IRQ, docs/mvp-isa.md `### EXEC`).
;
; How long the line stays low: `set pindirs, 1 [1]` is 2 SM cycles, `set x, 16` is 1, and
; the loop below retires in 32 SM cycles *seventeen* times -- `jmp x--` runs the n-th
; iteration with X = 0 as well, it just does not branch, and an instruction still costs its
; delay (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Instruction semantics`, JMP). So 2 + 1 + 17 x 32 = 547 SM
; cycles, which at CLKDIV 50 is 547 us. DS18B20 tRSTL is a 480 us minimum and AN126 H's
; maximum is 640 us, so the pulse sits inside the window with room on both sides. That
; 547 is also why this is a tight design: it is the longest single timing in the program,
; and it is what fixes the fastest legal divider at 47 (547 x 47 x 20 ns = 514 us).
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
reset:
    set pindirs, 1 [1]        ; pull the line low: a reset pulse starts
    set x, 16                 ; the trip count of the loop below
rst_low:
    jmp x-- rst_low [31]      ; 32 SM cycles, 17 times: see the header
    set pindirs, 0            ; release: the slave answers with its presence pulse
    ; The presence check. The pulse starts 15..60 us after this release and lasts
    ; 60..240 us, so a master that samples the line once has to do it where *every*
    ; legal pulse is still low: 60 us (the latest a pulse may start) to 75 us (the
    ; earliest any pulse may end, 15 + 60). The sample is 69 SM cycles after the release,
    ; which at CLKDIV 50 is 69 us, and it reads the pad three core cycles earlier, so it
    ; lands at 68.9 us: inside that window with room for a whole SM cycle of divider phase
    ; either side. A 0 in the RX FIFO therefore means "a device answered".
    nop [31]                  ; 32 SM cycles, out to 33 after the release
    nop [31]                  ; 32 SM cycles, out to 65
    nop [3]                   ; 4 SM cycles, out to 69
    in pins, 1 [4]            ; sample the line 69 SM cycles after the release
    push block                ; and hand the bit to the host. This is the only way the
                              ; host learns about it: there is no IRQ, no status bit for
                              ; a pin, and no way to write the ISR from outside
                              ; (docs/mvp-regmap.md `### Address map`).
    set x, 16                 ; the wait for tRSTH: 480 us is the minimum from the
rst_rsth:                    ; reset release to the first slot of the transaction,
    jmp x-- rst_rsth [31]     ; and 545 SM cycles is 545 us at CLKDIV 50
    ; falls through into the bit loop, which pulls the ROM command the host pushed

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The byte boundary, and the only place the engine ever stops: `pull block` stalls
; with the line released until the host pushes the next word. Every phase change goes
; through here, which is why the host can watch SM0_ADDR and know that no slot is half
; way through.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
w_byte:
    pull block

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; One time slot. See the header for why the two branches have to be the same length.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
w_slot:
    out x, 1 [1]              ; X = the OSR bit, OSR >>= 1, one slot counted
    jmp x-- w_zero            ; 1 = pull the line low (a 0-bit), 0 = release
w_one:                        ; a write-1 or a read slot: a short low, then idle
    set pindirs, 1 [5]        ; 6 SM cycles low: tLOW1 is 1..15 us
    set pindirs, 0 [5]        ; release, 6 SM cycles: the line is high from here on
    nop [9]                   ; 10 SM cycles: 16 SM cycles into the slot
    in pins, 1 [3]            ; sample 25 SM cycles in, i.e. 25 us at CLKDIV 50
    nop [31]                  ; 32 SM cycles: out to 61
    nop [11]                  ; 12 SM cycles: out to 73
    jmp w_join                ; the other branch is 73 SM cycles long as well
w_zero:                       ; a write-0: the line stays low for the whole low time
    set pindirs, 1 [5]        ; the same 6 SM cycles, so both slots start together
    nop [31]                  ; 32 SM cycles
    nop [26]                  ; 27 SM cycles: 65 SM cycles of low, i.e. 65 us, which
    set pindirs, 0 [5]        ; is inside tLOW0's 60..120 us, and tREC's 1 us minimum
w_join:
    jmp !osre w_slot          ; eight slots per word; then fall through to the next byte
next_byte:
    jmp w_byte                ; back to `pull block`: the engine stops here until the
                              ; host pushes the next word. This is the last instruction of
                              ; the program and the top of the wrap window, so a PC that ever
                              ; ran past it would land in `init`, the park, with the line
                              ; released -- a bus that reads high and nothing driving it.